      "2(b)(i) Depreciable cost = $224,000 − $24,000 = $200,000. Monthly depreciation = $200,000 ÷ 40 = $5,000; annual depreciation = $5,000 × 12 = $60,000. (ii) April 1 to December 31 is nine months, so first-year depreciation is $45,000. (iii) In the Depreciation Expense Account, debit $45,000 in 2004 against Provision for Depreciation and credit Profit and Loss $45,000. Repeat with $60,000 in 2005. If showing accumulated depreciation instead, it closes at credit $45,000 in 2004 and credit $105,000 in 2005; distinguish that balance from the year's expense.",
