    6: worked("Kingstown Domino Club: accounts and missing disposal information", [
      "6(a)(i) From the listed opening balances, accumulated fund = land $10,000 + refreshments $500 + debtors for tables $800 + bank $1,752 − subscriptions in advance $480 − refreshment creditors $1,820 = $10,752. This is the identifiable opening fund. The question also mentions old tables sold during the year without supplying their opening carrying amount; if they were held at January 1, their carrying amount must be added to this fund.",
      "6(a)(ii) Refreshment purchases = cash $3,800 + closing creditors $1,020 − opening creditors $1,820 = $3,000. Cost of sales = opening stock $500 + purchases $3,000 − closing stock $1,120 = $2,380; trading profit $3,368 − $2,380 = $988.",
      "6(a)(iii) Subscription income = $14,380 + opening advance $480 − closing advance $780 = $14,080. Tournament result is loss $7,200 ticket purchases − $800 sales = $6,400. Other revenue expenses are coach $2,000, rent $1,000, magazines $980, secretarial $1,880, cleaning $780, and depreciation on new tables $7,800 × 10% = $780, assuming a full year. Before any result on disposal of old tables, surplus = $14,080 + $988 − $6,400 − $7,420 = $1,248. Domino sets $3,000 and new tables $7,800 are capital purchases. The $7,600 table receipt includes collecting an opening $800 debtor, leaving $6,800 current disposal proceeds; only proceeds minus the disposed tables' carrying amount is disposal profit. Without that amount an unqualified final surplus cannot be determined.",
      "6(b)(i) The closing bank balance is on the receipts side of a balanced receipts/payments account, indicating an overdraft $540: show a current liability. (ii) Subscriptions in advance $780 are a current liability. (iii) A surplus increases the accumulated fund; a deficit reduces it.",
    ], ["No carrying amount of the old tables is supplied, so a complete opening accumulated fund and disposal result cannot be uniquely checked. The calculated $1,248 is explicitly before that disposal result; it is not asserted as an unconditional final surplus."]),
    7: worked("Maxie’s: ratios and interpretation", [
      "7(a) Current ratio = $15,010 ÷ $700 = 21.44:1. Acid test = ($15,010 − stock $810) ÷ $700 = 20.29:1 under the course formula excluding stock. Return on opening capital invested = net profit $29,860 ÷ $21,110 × 100 = 141.45%. Stock turnover = cost of sales $80,940 ÷ average stock (($680 + $810) ÷ 2 = $745) = 108.64 times. Net profit percentage = $29,860 ÷ $154,610 × 100 = 19.31%. Show the denominators used.",
      "7(b)(i) Net current assets are working capital. (ii) The $500 loan interest is an expense of the year and also an unpaid liability at year end, so it belongs in both statements for different reasons. (iii) A healthy current ratio indicates capacity to meet short-term liabilities from current assets, while an exceptionally high ratio can also suggest idle resources. (iv) $10,060 is carrying amount/net book value. (v) Accumulated depreciation is $22,890 − $10,060 = $12,830, exceeding this year's $3,620 charge; earlier charges are therefore indicated.",
      "7(c) (i) False: 1:5 means only $0.20 of quick assets per $1 current liability. (ii) True: 2:4 simplifies to 0.5:1. (iii) True: acquiring a fixed asset is capital expenditure. (iv) False: prepayments relate to later periods and are not all this year's expense. (v) True: writing off a debt charges the loss to profit and reduces the receivable. Do not confuse the printed colon ratios with decimal 1.5:1 or 2.4:1.",
    ], ["Some quick-ratio policies also exclude prepayments: ($15,010 − $810 − $1,350) ÷ $700 = 18.36:1. An explicitly stated version is defensible. Return on capital can use another clearly defined base, but this solution uses the supplied opening capital rather than silently mixing bases."]),
